\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 1. Agent State \'97 Recap & Reinforcement\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 State
\f1\b0  = a 
\f4\fs26 TypedDict
\f1\fs24  that holds the agent's 
\f0\b conversation history
\f1\b0  (
\f4\fs26 messages
\f1\fs24  field by default) plus any 
\f0\b custom fields
\f1\b0  the developer adds for tools/middleware to use.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You extend it by 
\f0\b inheriting from 
\f5\fs26 AgentState
\f1\b0\fs24  and adding your own fields (e.g., a 
\f4\fs26 model_call_count
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 is_vip_member
\f1\fs24 , etc. \'97 examples reused from earlier middleware lectures).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Node-style middleware hooks
\f1\b0  (
\f4\fs26 before_model
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 after_model
\f1\fs24 ) receive this 
\f0\b state
\f1\b0  object as a parameter, and can return a dict of updates that get merged back into it.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Key clarification: there is 
\f0\b no separate "conversation history" variable
\f1\b0  \'97 conversation history simply 
\f2\i lives inside
\f1\i0  
\f4\fs26 state["messages"]
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Agent Runtime \'97 The New Deep-Dive Topic\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 2.1 The problem runtime solves\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 An agent often needs information that is 
\f0\b not part of the conversation
\f1\b0  itself: 
\f2\i who
\f1\i0  is talking (user ID, VIP status), 
\f2\i where
\f1\i0  (which cinema/city/geography), auth/session info, etc.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Analogy: a 
\f0\b hospital wristband
\f1\b0  \'97 it carries a patient's identity/room/history so any department (ER, pharmacist, nurse) can look them up without the patient re-explaining everything each time. Runtime is the "wristband" for an agent.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This is distinct from short-term memory (state/messages) and from long-term memory (the store) \'97 it's contextual/session information injected from outside the conversation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.2 What runtime is, mechanically\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f4\b0\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 create_agent
\f1\fs24  (LangChain) actually runs on 
\f0\b LangGraph's runtime
\f1\b0  under the hood \'97 LangGraph sits 
\f2\i below
\f1\i0  LangChain (hierarchy: LangGraph 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  LangChain 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  Deep Agents).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Every running agent (like every running program) maintains a runtime \'97 internal bookkeeping about the current execution.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangGraph's runtime object exposes 5 things:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Context
\f1\b0  \'97 static, per-invocation info: user ID, DB connections, other dependencies/config.\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Store
\f1\b0  \'97 long-term memory that survives across separate sessions (e.g., an 
\f4\fs26 InMemoryStore
\f1\fs24  or a Postgres-backed store).\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stream Writer
\f1\b0  \'97 a channel for emitting custom progress updates while the agent runs (separate from the final conversation output).\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Execution Info
\f1\b0  \'97 thread ID, run ID, retry/attempt number (bookkeeping for a specific run/node in the underlying graph).\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Server Info
\f1\b0  \'97 metadata relevant only when running on a LangGraph 
\f2\i server
\f1\i0  deployment (otherwise 
\f4\fs26 None
\f1\fs24 ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.3 Defining and using a context schema\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs26 \cf0 from dataclasses import dataclass\
\
@dataclass\
class CinebotContext:\
    username: str\
    geo_state: str  # e.g. "Delhi", "Bangalore"\
\
agent_with_context = create_agent(\
    model="openai:gpt-5-mini",\
    context_schema=CinebotContext,\
)\
\
agent_with_context.invoke(\
    \{"messages": [\{"role": "user", "content": "What's my name?"\}]\},\
    context=CinebotContext(username="Mayank", geo_state="Delhi"),\
)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Passing 
\f4\fs26 context=
\f1\fs24  does 
\f0\b not automatically get "used"
\f1\b0  by the model \'97 it's just made available; you still have to explicitly read and apply it somewhere (a tool, or a middleware hook) for it to have an effect.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.4 How runtime reaches Tools \'97 
\f5\fs30\fsmilli15210 ToolRuntime
\f0\fs28 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When a tool needs runtime info, LangChain passes it a special 
\f5\b\fs26 tool_runtime
\f1\b0\fs24  parameter (its own type, separate from the plain agent runtime) \'97 this happens 
\f0\b automatically/implicitly
\f1\b0 ; you don't manually plumb it through.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 tool_runtime
\f1\fs24  bundles: 
\f0\b state
\f1\b0 , the same 5 runtime pieces (context, store, stream_writer, execution_info, server_info), plus extras like 
\f4\fs26 tool_call_id
\f1\fs24  and 
\f4\fs26 config
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 There is 
\f0\b no other way
\f1\b0  to access the store/context from inside a tool other than through this 
\f4\fs26 tool_runtime
\f1\fs24  parameter \'97 you cannot just call something like 
\f4\fs26 loyalty_store.get(...)
\f1\fs24  directly; you must go through 
\f4\fs26 runtime.store...
\f1\fs24  inside the tool.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Example: a 
\f4\fs26 fetch_customer_preferences
\f1\fs24  tool reads 
\f4\fs26 runtime.context.user_id
\f1\fs24 , then looks up long-term preferences via 
\f4\fs26 runtime.store.get(("preferences",), user_id)
\f1\fs24  from an 
\f4\fs26 InMemoryStore
\f1\fs24  set up on the agent (
\f4\fs26 store=loyalty_store
\f1\fs24  param on 
\f4\fs26 create_agent
\f1\fs24 ) \'97 demonstrating cross-session personalization (e.g., Mayank123 likes horror/thriller; Priya1234 likes romance/thriller) without the user ever re-stating their preferences.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Declaring 
\f4\fs26 runtime: ToolRuntime
\f1\fs24  (or even naming the parameter anything, e.g. 
\f4\fs26 a, b
\f1\fs24  \'97 LangChain matches by position/type, not name) inside a 
\f4\fs26 @tool
\f1\fs24 -decorated function is 
\f0\b optional
\f1\b0  \'97 a tool that doesn't declare it simply won't fail, it just won't receive runtime info.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.5 How runtime reaches Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Node-style hooks
\f1\b0  (
\f4\fs26 before_model
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 after_model
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 before_agent
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 after_agent
\f1\fs24 ) are called by LangChain with 
\f5\b\fs26 (state, runtime)
\f1\b0\fs24  as positional arguments \'97 this is 
\f0\b mandatory
\f1\b0 , not optional. If you define the decorated function without accepting these two parameters, LangChain raises a 
\f4\fs26 TypeError
\f1\fs24  (demonstrated live: 
\f4\fs26 log_before_model() takes 0 positional arguments but 2 were given
\f1\fs24 ).\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Wrap-style hooks
\f1\b0  (
\f4\fs26 wrap_model_call
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 wrap_tool_call
\f1\fs24 ) instead receive a 
\f5\b\fs26 request
\f1\b0\fs24  object (
\f4\fs26 ModelRequest
\f1\fs24  or 
\f4\fs26 ToolCallRequest
\f1\fs24 ) 
\f0\b and a 
\f5\fs26 handler
\f1\b0\fs24  callable; the request object itself carries 
\f4\fs26 .runtime
\f1\fs24  and 
\f4\fs26 .state
\f1\fs24  as attributes, plus (for tools) the tool call's name/arguments. The hook 
\f0\b must return a response
\f1\b0  \'97 you can't just 
\f4\fs26 pass
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Practical worked example \'97 
\f0\b dynamic/personalized system prompt
\f1\b0  via 
\f4\fs26 @dynamic_prompt
\f1\fs24  (a 
\f4\fs26 wrap_model_call
\f1\fs24 -style decorator from 
