\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) \'97 Deeper Pass\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 (Builds on the HITL middleware covered in the earlier middleware lectures \'97 approve/edit/reject/respond. This session goes further into 
\f0\b mechanics, conditional triggering, and the "respond" decision
\f1\b0 .)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.1 Recap of the four decisions\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Approve
\f1\b0  \'97 run the tool call as-is.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Edit
\f1\b0  \'97 modify the arguments before running.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reject
\f1\b0  \'97 skip execution, agent gets a rejection message.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Respond
\f1\b0  \'97 human sends free-text back to the agent as if it were a tool/synthetic result, 
\f0\b without executing
\f1\b0  the tool \'97 used for "ask the user a clarifying question" style flows.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.2 Reading interrupts programmatically\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 After invoking an agent whose flow paused, check 
\f5\b\fs26 result.interrupts
\f1\b0\fs24  (or the equivalent field on the returned state) to detect that the agent is waiting for human input \'97 this is how a UI knows whether the agent finished normally or is paused waiting on you.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Each interrupt carries: 
\f4\fs26 action_request
\f1\fs24  
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  tool 
\f4\fs26 name
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 arguments
\f1\fs24 , a 
\f4\fs26 description
\f1\fs24  of what's pending approval, and the 
\f4\fs26 allowed_decisions
\f1\fs24  list for that specific interrupt.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Common pitfall demonstrated live: forgetting to 
\f0\b redefine the agent with a fresh/matching 
\f5\fs26 thread_id
\f1\b0\fs24  before invoking again causes stale state and missing interrupts \'97 always ensure a checkpointer + consistent 
\f4\fs26 thread_id
\f1\fs24  across the pause/resume pair.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.3 Resuming with 
\f5\fs30\fsmilli15210 Command
\f0\fs28 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs26 \cf0 from langgraph.types import Command\
\
agent.invoke(Command(resume=\{"decision": "approve"\}), config=config)\
# or: \{"decision": "edit", ...\}, \{"decision": "reject", "reason": "..."\},\
#     \{"decision": "respond", "message": "..."\}\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This is the same 
\f4\fs26 Command(resume=...)
\f1\fs24  mechanism introduced earlier in the course for resuming an interrupted graph \'97 reused here in more depth.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reject
\f1\b0  demo: configuring a stricter policy (e.g., "cancellation requires manager sign-off; ask the customer to call support") \'97 the agent explains this back to the user rather than cancelling.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Respond
\f1\b0  demo: a dedicated 
\f4\fs26 ask_customer
\f1\fs24  tool with 
\f4\fs26 interrupt_on=\{"ask_customer": \{"allowed_decisions": ["respond"]\}\}
\f1\fs24  lets the agent ask a clarifying question (e.g., "Do you want a refund too?") and receive a free-text human answer that feeds back into the conversation 
\f2\i without
\f1\i0  being an approve/edit/reject verdict \'97 i.e., HITL isn't only about gatekeeping actions, it can also make the human a collaborative participant mid-task (this is effectively how Claude's own clarifying-question behavior works).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.4 Conditional interrupts (the advanced/interview-relevant part)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Real business rule used as the running example: 
\f2\i "Only require human approval to cancel a booking if the refund amount exceeds $100 \'97 otherwise auto-approve."
\f1\i0  (Explicitly framed as a realistic Amazon/Flipkart/Swiggy interview-style requirement: "if order value > 
\f6 \uc0\u8377 
\f1 10,000, require executive confirmation.")\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Implemented via the 
\f4\fs26 interrupt_on
\f1\fs24  middleware config accepting a 
\f0\b callable predicate
\f1\b0  instead of a static bool/config, operating on a 
\f5\b\fs26 ToolCallRequest
\f1\b0\fs24 : 
\f4\fs26 def is_large_refund(request: ToolCallRequest) -> bool:    amount = request.tool_call["args"].get("amount", 0)    return amount > 100create_agent(    model=model,    tools=[cancel_booking],    middleware=[        HumanInTheLoopMiddleware(            interrupt_on=\{"cancel_booking": \{"allowed_decisions": [...], "when": is_large_refund\}\}        )    ],    checkpointer=checkpointer,)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \uc0\u8232 
\f2\i\fs24 (Exact parameter name given in the live session was 
\f7\fs26 when
\f2\fs24 , taking a callable that receives the 
\f7\fs26 tool_call_request
\f2\fs24  and returns 
\f7\fs26 True
\f2\fs24  to interrupt / 
\f7\fs26 False
\f2\fs24  to auto-approve.)
\f1\i0 \
\ls12\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ToolCallRequest
\f1\fs24  (received by 
\f4\fs26 wrap_tool_call
\f1\fs24  
\f0\b and
\f1\b0  by this predicate) contains: the 
\f4\fs26 tool_call
\f1\fs24  (name + arguments), 
\f4\fs26 tool
\f1\fs24 , 
\f4\fs26 state
\f1\fs24 , and 
\f4\fs26 runtime
\f1\fs24  \'97 same building blocks as elsewhere, letting the condition be based on argument values, state, or runtime/context data.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Demonstrated: a $25 refund request 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  
\f0\b no interrupt
\f1\b0 , auto-approved and executed immediately; a larger refund 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  
\f0\b interrupt fires
\f1\b0 , execution pauses exactly like the static case.\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 General takeaway: 
\f0\b HITL doesn't have to be all-or-nothing
\f1\b0  \'97 it can be 
\f0\b data-driven
\f1\b0 , deciding per-call whether human oversight is actually warranted, which is closer to how production systems (Amazon/Flipkart-style approval gates) actually work.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.5 Putting it together \'97 an interactive HITL loop (UI-style pattern, reused from earlier)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The recurring helper pattern from previous lectures was reused: invoke the agent 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  check for 
\f4\fs26 interrupts
\f1\fs24  
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  if present, prompt the human (via 
\f4\fs26 input()
\f1\fs24  in the demo, or a real UI button in production) for their decision 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  build the appropriate 
\f4\fs26 Command(resume=...)
\f1\fs24  
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  invoke again 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  repeat until no interrupts remain.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Framed explicitly as: 
\f2\i "This is exactly how Claude/ChatGPT and other agentic products implement their approval prompts under the hood \'97 same concept, nicer UI."
\f1\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Key Takeaways / Recurring Instructor Emphasis\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Runtime and state are not optional trivia
\f1\b0  \'97 they are the mechanism by which tools and middleware get 
\f2\i anything
\f1\i0  beyond raw conversation text (auth, personalization, long-term memory access, conditional business logic). Understanding them deeply is what separates "I can build an agent" from "I can build a production-grade, controllable agent."\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 
\f0\b passing of 
\f5\fs26 state
\f0\fs24 /
\f5\fs26 runtime
\f0\fs24 /
\f5\fs26 tool_runtime
\f0\fs24 /
\f5\fs26 request
\f0\fs24  is controlled entirely by the framework (LangChain/LangGraph)
\f1\b0  \'97 the developer's job is only to 
\f2\i declare
\f1\i0  the right parameter shape and 
\f2\i use
\f1\i0  what's handed to them; you cannot manually fetch a store/context object out of thin air.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Conditional Human-in-the-Loop
\f1\b0  is presented as a realistic, interview-grade skill (tying a business threshold, like order value, to whether a human gate is triggered) rather than a purely academic middleware feature.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The instructor deliberately timed this "runtime + HITL" refresher 
\f0\b right before MCP
\f1\b0 , because MCP will require students to be fluent in these same underlying agent-execution concepts, just applied to a new, framework-independent protocol.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP will be taught as a genuine detour: full protocol/architecture, building an MCP 
\f0\b server
\f1\b0  and 
\f0\b client
\f1\b0  from scratch, and publishing to the cloud \'97 
\f2\i before
\f1\i0  returning to "how do I plug an MCP server into my LangChain agent." Estimated at 3+ classes minimum.\
}